uidance.\n  - Orders were up 11% year on year, led by utility and data-centre customers.\n  - Cancellations were below 1%, so the backlog looks firm.\n  - Some backlog will convert in 2026 and beyond, so the 1.2x overstates what is left for 2027.\n- **Run rate:**\n  - Quarterly revenue (C$M) was 905 in Q3 2025, 960 in Q4 2025, 980 in Q1 2026 and 890 in Q2 2026.\n  - Q2 2026 fell because of the Halbrook fire. Halbrook is the largest plant and was offline for nine weeks.\n  - The trailing four quarters sum to about C$3.74B. Reaching C$4.2B needs about 12% growth over that, or an average of about C$1.05B a quarter. That is about 7% above the Q1 2026 peak.\n- **Operations:**\n  - Halbrook restarted in August at full capacity.\n  - Management expects to recover the lost shipments by mid-2027. That means some catch-up volume lands in H1 2027, which helps the 2027 total.\n  - It also means capacity is tight until then. Meeting the guidance depends on output running above the recent rate.\n\n**Implication:** Demand and backlog support the guidance, and the main risk is execution. My estimate for 2027 revenue is about C$4.0–4.2B, with the guidance reachable if the Halbrook ramp and catch-up go well. I did not find disclosures on pricing, or on how much of the backlog is scheduled for 2027 versus 2026. Those are the largest gaps in this estimate.","ttft_ms":2757,"type":"result","duration_ms":30834,"uuid":"bdebe5db-1a5d-4546-a1d3-0eb55a7ab818","ttft_stream_ms":2530,"time_to_request_ms":85,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":10,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":1,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":11,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":2,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":8,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":9,"request_build":16},"turn_start_resume_k